WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.220
When a receptor grips a ligand that is stuck to another cell, which cell gets the

2
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.940
message? The receptor sits in the upper cell.

3
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.220
Its ligand is held in the membrane of the lower cell.

4
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:16.740
Does the message reach one of them, or both?

5
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:18.440
Often, both.

6
00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:22.010
Many ligands are made as membrane proteins.

7
00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:25.290
Such a ligand can have a tail inside its own cell.

8
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.850
A tail in the cytosol is what you expect of a receptor.

9
00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:33.510
When the two proteins bind, the receptor signals into its own cell.

10
00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:35.310
That is the forward signal.

11
00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:38.950
A second message passes into the cell that carries the ligand.

12
00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:40.870
That is reverse signaling.

13
00:00:42.883 --> 00:00:45.443
Ephrins are the clearest case of reverse signaling.

14
00:00:46.023 --> 00:00:50.043
A B -type efren crosses the membrane once and ends in a short tail.

15
00:00:50.603 --> 00:00:52.363
The tail contains no enzyme.

16
00:00:52.643 --> 00:00:57.063
Yet when the efren meets the outer part of its receptor, its tail gains a phosphate

17
00:00:57.063 --> 00:01:02.483
on a tyrosine. That outer part has no kinase, and neither does the efren, So a

18
00:01:02.483 --> 00:01:05.243
kinase in the ephrin's own cell must do the work.

19
00:01:06.017 --> 00:01:11.697
Which kinase? SARC family kinases gather where B -type ephrins cluster, and they

20
00:01:11.697 --> 00:01:13.477
help to put phosphate on the tail.

21
00:01:13.977 --> 00:01:16.897
An adaptor then binds the phosphorylated tyrosines.

22
00:01:17.597 --> 00:01:20.997
It connects the ephrin to proteins that reshape the actin skeleton.

23
00:01:21.857 --> 00:01:25.477
This is one route; PDZ proteins offer another.

24
00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:32.210
Suppose you delete the gene for a B -type ephron, and an organ fails to form.

25
00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:36.770
You have removed the forward signal, the reverse signal, and the grip that held the

26
00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:40.850
cells together. The knockout cannot tell you which loss caused the defect.

27
00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:43.050
So change the ephrin instead.

28
00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:47.490
Keep its outer part and its membrane segment, and remove only the tail.

29
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.250
Check that it reaches the surface, then measure the forward signal.

30
00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.010
Reverse signaling through the tail is gone.

31
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.060
Other ligands work this way too.

32
00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.300
Membrane bound TNF acts as a receptor, and so does a fly semaphorin.

33
00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:07.380
In each pair, the protein named receptor also gives a signal.

34
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.360
The protein named ligand also receives one.

35
00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.420
The names were fixed by history.

36
00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:17.760
So keep this rule: a membrane ligand can receive a signal as well as give one.
